<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01//ES"
        "http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/strict.dtd">

<html>
  <head>
    <title>Sgt. Mayor - Get your tricks or regret it</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Documentation of the Sgt. Mayor cardgame">
    <meta name="keywords" content="Documentation, Docu, Sgt. Mayor; game, card, cardgame">

    <meta name="DC.Creator" content="Markus Schwab">
    <meta name="DC.Date" content="2004-04-18">
    <meta name="DC.Rights" content="Copyright (C) 2004 - 2008; distributed under the GNU Free Documentation License">
  </head>

  <body>
    <h1 align="center">Sgt. Mayor</h1>
    <hr size=2>

    <p>Sgt. Mayor is an english cardgame - or at least I learned it from two
      english people (or actually from Ver&oacute;nica (who is not exactly
      english, but learned it herself from those two english. But who cares
      ...)). It is also known as 3-5-8 (though the version here is
      rather its 6-5-8 variant).</p>

    <p>This is a trick-taking card game, in which each player has to
      win at least a certain number of tricks. The player winning at
      first 10<a href="#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a> tricks more than
      needed (cummulated) wins the game.</>

    <h2>Dealing</h2>
    <p>The game is played by 3 persons with a standard 52-cards deck
      having the 2 of clubs (&clubs;-2) removed. Each player gets 17
      cards.</p>

    <p>The first game is started by a randomly selected player
      (needing 6 tricks). The next ones are started by the others in
      turn.</p>

    <h2>Playing</h2>
    <p>The players have to get 6, 3 and 8 tricks respectively. The
      player needing 8 tricks starts by selecting the colour of the
      trumps (by clicking a card with that suit). Then the actual game
      starts according to the following rules:</p>

    <p>Each turn can be started by any colour. The other players
      continue counter-clockwise following suit, or playing any card
      (be it a trump or not) if unable to do so.</p>

    <p>Each trick is won by the highest trump, or by the highest card
      of the suit led if no trump card was played to the trick. The
      winner of each trick leads to the next.</p>

    <p>The difference between the made and the proposed tricks is
      added to the score. The player reaching first 10<a
      href="#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a> wins the game.</p>

    <p>The next game starts the player which before needed 6 tricks.
      Now he must get 8 tricks.</p>

    <h2>Exchange</h2>
    <p>Before starting the next game (actually naming the next
      trump), there's an exchange of cards.</p>

    <p>Each player who made more than needed tricks, can give one
      unwanted card per overtrick to a player who didn't fullfill his
      needed tricks and gets in return those player highest card of
      the same suit (or any card, if they don't any of those).</p>

    <p>If two players won overticks, the player with the higher amount
      of needed cards for the hand about to be played trades
      first.</p>

    <p>The status line gives some information about what's going on!</p>

    <h2>Note to the layout</h2>
    <p>After starting a game you have (from left to right and from top to
      bottom):</p>

    <ul>
      <li>A card (an ace) indicating the special colour.</li>

      <li>The cards of the human player. The won cards (above) become visible,
        if they are clicked into; right-clicking allows to sort them.</li>

      <li>The played pile.</li>

      <li>In the last line there are players 1 and 2</li>
    </ul>

    <hr size=1 noshade>
    <p style="text-indent:-0.3cm;margin-left:0.3cm"><a name="Note1"></a>1)
      This number can be changed in the settings dialog.</p>

    <hr size=3 noshade>
